    "module": "Nigerian Political History",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Military rule</span> is a system in which the armed forces seize power, usually by a coup d'état, and govern the country. Nigeria was under military rule for about 29 of its years since independence, from the first coup in January 1966 to the return to civil rule in May 1999.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Reasons for Military Intervention</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Political corruption and misrule</td><td>Civilian leaders were accused of embezzlement, nepotism and abuse of office.</td></tr><tr><td>Political instability</td><td>Crises such as the 1962–64 Western Region disturbances and disputed elections.</td></tr><tr><td>Ethnic and regional rivalry</td><td>Fears of domination by one region or ethnic group.</td></tr><tr><td>Economic mismanagement</td><td>Inflation, unemployment and poor handling of the economy.</td></tr><tr><td>Personal ambition</td><td>Some officers wanted power and its benefits.</td></tr><tr><td>Weak democratic institutions</td><td>Rigged elections and lack of respect for the rule of law invited intervention.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Structure of Government Under Military Rule</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>The constitution was suspended or modified, and government ruled by <strong>decrees</strong>.</li><li>A supreme body (Supreme Military Council, Armed Forces Ruling Council or Provisional Ruling Council) made policy.</li><li>The Federal Executive Council (FEC) handled day-to-day administration.</li><li>Military governors, later joined by civilian commissioners, ran the states.</li><li>The legislature was dissolved and political parties banned; the judiciary was limited in what it could challenge.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Performance of Military Administrations</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Achievements</th><th>Shortcomings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Won the civil war and reunited Nigeria (1967–70); creation of states; the 3Rs programme</td><td>Human rights abuses, detention without trial, press censorship</td></tr><tr><td>Development projects: roads, universities, oil-boom infrastructure, Abuja</td><td>Corruption and misuse of oil revenue</td></tr><tr><td>Handovers to civilians in 1979 and 1999</td><td>Annulment of the June 12, 1993 election; prolonged transitions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Ways of Avoiding Military Coups</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Practise good, transparent and accountable governance; fight corruption.</li><li>Hold free and fair elections and respect the rule of law.</li><li>Ensure equitable distribution of resources and fairness among ethnic groups.</li><li>Keep the military professional, well paid and under firm civilian control.</li><li>Educate citizens and the military on the value of democracy; strengthen democratic institutions.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the sequence of regimes: Ironsi (Jan 1966), Gowon (1966–75), Murtala/Obasanjo (1975–79), Buhari (1983–85), Babangida (1985–93), Abacha (1993–98), Abdulsalami (1998–99).</div>",
