    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">conflict</span> is a disagreement or clash between individuals, groups or nations arising from differences in interests, values, beliefs or goals. Conflict is a normal part of social life, but it becomes harmful when handled badly.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Causes of Conflict</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Competition over scarce resources</td><td>Land, oil, water, grazing routes</td></tr><tr><td>Ethnic and religious differences</td><td>Intolerance and marginalisation</td></tr><tr><td>Political power struggles</td><td>Election disputes, rigging, succession crises</td></tr><tr><td>Boundary and territorial disputes</td><td>Bakassi dispute between Nigeria and Cameroon</td></tr><tr><td>Injustice and inequality</td><td>Perceived unfair distribution of wealth and opportunity</td></tr><tr><td>Poor communication and misunderstanding</td><td>Rumour and mistrust</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Consequences of Conflict</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Loss of lives and property; injuries and displacement of people.</li><li>Refugee problems and humanitarian crises.</li><li>Poverty, hunger and disruption of economic activities.</li><li>Breakdown of law and order; distrust between groups.</li><li>Poor image of the country and reduced foreign investment.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Ways of Resolving and Managing Conflict</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Negotiation</td><td>Parties in dispute discuss directly to reach agreement.</td></tr><tr><td>Mediation</td><td>A neutral third party helps the parties reach a settlement but cannot impose it.</td></tr><tr><td>Conciliation</td><td>A third party works to reduce tension and bring the parties together.</td></tr><tr><td>Arbitration</td><td>A neutral arbiter hears both sides and gives a decision, often binding.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjudication</td><td>The dispute is settled by a court of law.</td></tr><tr><td>Diplomacy / dialogue</td><td>Peaceful talks, especially between nations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Note:</strong> Mediation is advisory, arbitration is usually binding, and adjudication is by a court.</div>",
