    "topic": "International Organisation: United Nations Organisation (UNO)",
    "topicCode": "SS3-GOV-07",
    "module": "International Organisations",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">United Nations Organisation (UNO)</span> is a global body of sovereign states formed in 1945, after the Second World War, to maintain international peace and security. It replaced the League of Nations and has its headquarters in New York.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Origin</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">The UN Charter was signed in San Francisco in June 1945 and the organisation came into being on 24 October 1945 with 51 original members. Nigeria joined in 1960 as its 99th member.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Principal Organs</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General Assembly</td><td>All members; discusses issues and passes the budget</td></tr><tr><td>Security Council</td><td>Primary responsibility for peace and security; 5 permanent members with veto (USA, UK, France, Russia, China) and 10 non-permanent</td></tr><tr><td>Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC)</td><td>Economic, social and humanitarian cooperation</td></tr><tr><td>Trusteeship Council</td><td>Supervised trust territories (suspended operations in 1994)</td></tr><tr><td>International Court of Justice (ICJ)</td><td>Settles legal disputes between states; sits at The Hague</td></tr><tr><td>Secretariat</td><td>Administration, headed by the Secretary-General</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Aims and Objectives</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Maintain international peace and security.</li><li>Develop friendly relations among nations.</li><li>Promote cooperation in solving economic, social, cultural and humanitarian problems.</li><li>Promote respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Achievements and Problems</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Achievements</th><th>Problems</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peacekeeping and conflict prevention</td><td>The veto power of the five permanent members</td></tr><tr><td>Decolonisation of many territories</td><td>Shortage of funds and unpaid contributions</td></tr><tr><td>Specialised agencies: WHO, UNESCO, UNICEF, FAO, ILO</td><td>Inability to enforce decisions against powerful states</td></tr><tr><td>Humanitarian aid and refugee support</td><td>Domination by big powers; slow response to crises</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
